      "chromatophore": "Chromatophore",
      "chromatophore_2": "Chromatophore",
      "chromatophore_color_study": "Chromatophore color study",
      "chromatophore_iridophore_leucophore_pa": "Chromatophore + iridophore + leucophore + papillae coordination is controlled by ~38 distinct neural lobes in the cephalopod brain. The chromatic system is among the most-studied invertebrate motor systems.",
      "chromatophores_are_individual_speciali": "Chromatophores are individual specialized cells in cephalopod skin that change color by changing SIZE. Each chromatophore consists of a central pigment-filled sac (the cytoelastic sacculus) surrounded by 15-25 radially-arranged muscle fibers, each attached to the cell wall by a tendon-like structure. When the muscle fibers contract simultaneously — under direct neural control from motor neurons in the chromatophore lobes of the cephalopod brain — they stretch the sac to up to 15 times its resting diameter, exposing more pigment to view. When the muscles relax, the elasticity of the sac wall snaps it back to a small dot. Most cephalopod skin has multiple LAYERS of chromatophores: a superficial yellow-orange layer, a middle red layer, and a deep brown-black layer. Different combinations of layer activations produce different colors (e.g., red + black = dark red; yellow + red = orange). The whole system is under direct neural control with very fast (under 100 ms) activation times. There are typically 100-250 chromatophores per square millimeter of cuttlefish skin — meaning a full-grown specimen has millions of these color cells. Cephalopods can switch full-body patterns in less than 200 milliseconds — faster than a human eye blink. This makes the cephalopod skin one of the highest-bandwidth display systems in biology.\n\nThe NETWORK level is equally remarkable. Skin patches are organized into \"morphological units\" — groups of chromatophores that activate together. Hanlon's lab has cataloged ~30 distinct morphological units in Sepia officinalis (cuttlefish). Different combinations of these units produce hundreds of distinct body patterns. The brain orchestrates which units to activate based on visual feedback from substrate, posture relative to surroundings, and possibly skin opsin \"sensing\" of ambient light. The result: a real-time adaptive display that integrates visual + tactile + thermal information into a coherent matching response. (Sources: Hanlon, 2007; Hanlon & Messenger, 2018; Florey, 1969.)",
      "chromatophores_iridophores_leucophores": "Chromatophores + iridophores + leucophores let them change color in milliseconds. They're mostly color-blind themselves, possibly seeing color via skin-distributed opsins.",
      "chromatophores_pigment_cells": "Chromatophores (pigment cells)",
      "circadian_rhythms_of_activity_in_the_c": "Circadian rhythms of activity in the cuttlefish Sepia officinalis",
      "circular_discoloration_patches_on_rock": "Circular discoloration patches on rocks or piers — left by recent contact. Sucker scars on prey shells or fish skin also indicate recent cephalopod activity.",
