      "hero_capitals": "Spelling is forgiving: accents and punctuation are ignored. Some countries split their capital — South Africa has three (Pretoria, Cape Town, Bloemfontein), Bolivia two (Sucre, La Paz), and the Netherlands governs from The Hague while Amsterdam is the constitutional capital. Those alternates are accepted.",
      "hero_continents": "The 7-continent model (US/UK): Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe, North America, South America. Many countries teach 6 (one America) or 5 (Eurasia). Here Australia and the Pacific islands are grouped as Oceania, and Antarctica has no countries. Russia and Turkey straddle two continents.",
      "hero_dist_hunt": "No score here. Slide to a distance, see which real capital-to-capital journeys are about that far, and note how confident you feel. The farthest two places on Earth can be is 20,037 km — half the circumference.",
      "hero_distance": "Capital to capital along the great circle (the haversine formula). New York to Tokyo is about 10,850 km over the Arctic — shorter than the flat-map straight line. Anchors: London–Paris 340 km, New York–Los Angeles 3,940 km, London–Sydney 17,000 km.",
      "hero_find_country": "Click the country on the map. Easy zooms to its continent and names countries on hover; Hard gives no hints. The map is a Mercator projection, which stretches land near the poles — Greenland looks as big as Africa but is 14× smaller.",
      "hero_globe": "Earth is an oblate spheroid — about 43 km wider across the equator than from pole to pole. The shortest path between two cities is a great-circle arc, not a straight line on a flat map, which is why flights bend over the Arctic.",
      "hero_landmarks": "Eiffel Tower (Paris), Christ the Redeemer (Rio), Sydney Opera House, Petra (Jordan), Angkor Wat (Cambodia). A vivid place gives memory a second handle on the country (dual coding). Some landmarks sit on a border — either country is accepted.",
      "hero_quiz_builder": "Describe a topic and get 10 short-answer questions pitched at your grade. Retrieval practice — pulling an answer from memory — beats re-reading for long-term recall (Karpicke & Roediger, 2008).",
